<h1>treap</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="binarySearchTree.html"><em>binary search tree</em></a> in which <a href="node.html"><em>nodes</em></a> have another <a href="key.html"><em>key</em></a>, called the priority.  Operations also keep the nodes <a href="heapproperty.html"><em>heap ordered</em></a> with regard to the priority.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="binarySearchTree.html"><em>binary search tree</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="randomizedBinarySearchTree.html"><em>randomized binary search tree</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
The name comes from "<a href="tree.html"><em>tree</em></a>" and "<a href="heap.html"><em>heap</em></a>". </p> 
<p> Some call "randomized binary search trees" treaps, but strictly a treap does not define how priorities are assigned.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
Oleg Kiselyov's <a href="https://okmij.org/ftp/Scheme/lib/treap.scm" target="_blank">(Scheme)</a> and <a href="https://okmij.org/ftp/Scheme/#treaps" target="_blank">verification code and other links</a>.
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<strong>Raimund G. Seidel</strong> and <strong>Cecilia R. Aragon</strong>,  <em>Randomized Search Trees</em>, Algorithmica, 16:464-497 (1996).   Also in 30th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, pages 540-545, Research Triangle Park, North Carolina, 30 October-1 November 1989. IEEE.
</p>

